(A list is handed to a function, the function appends a text to it, and the
 caller reads the list back. LANGUAGE.md: the compiler earns the homogeneous
 fast path by proof, and a value whose type it cannot statically prove widens
 the list to mixed - so a read dispatches on the slot's own tag. The pre-scan
 used to decide each function's lists in isolation, so the caller proved a
 list homogeneous that the callee had widened through the parameter, and
 `'s last` rendered the text's address as a number. docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #97.)

To 'note whatever' with a list called noted and a text called label.
  append label to noted.

a list called noted is [].
'note whatever' of noted and "tail".
Print "last: {noted's last}".

(The same append written at the caller - the neighbour that always worked.)
a list called written is [].
append "tail" to written.
Print "written: {written's last}".
